.TH ESDM\-VHOST\-USER\-RNG 8 "2026-10-19" "ESDM @VERSION@" "ESDM Manual"
.SH NAME
esdm\-vhost\-user\-rng \- vhost\-user back\-end of a virtio entropy device served from the ESDM
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B esdm\-vhost\-user\-rng
.RB { \-\-socket
.IR PATH " | "
.B \-\-fd
.IR N " | socket activation}"
.RB [ \-\-mode
.IR MODE ]
.RB [ \-\-socket_group
.IR GROUP ]
.RB [ \-\-pr ]
.RB [ \-\-max_bytes
.IR N
.RB [ \-\-period_ms
.IR MS ]]
.RB [ \-\-user
.IR USER ]
.RB [ \-\-group
.IR GROUP ]
.RB [ \-\-syslog ]
.RB [ \-v ]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B esdm\-vhost\-user\-rng
gives a virtual machine a virtio entropy device (virtio\-rng, virtio device
ID 4) whose random bytes come from the ESDM. It is the back\-end of the
vhost\-user protocol: the VMM \- QEMU or Cloud Hypervisor \- connects to its
UNIX domain socket, shares the memory of the guest with it and hands it the
request queue of the device, and the daemon serves the requests of the guest's
driver directly from that memory, without the VMM in between.
.PP
The daemon obtains the random bytes from the ESDM over its unprivileged RPC
interface, like any other client of
.BR libesdm_rpc_client (3).
It parses memory the guest writes, the most hostile input the ESDM has to deal
with anywhere, and therefore is a process of its own rather than part of
.BR esdm\-server (8),
and one process per VM: a guest that took over its back\-end has gained what
any local user has \- the unprivileged RPC interface \- and neither the ESDM nor
the back\-end of another guest.
.SS Random bytes
By default, a request is served from the fully seeded DRNG of the ESDM
(esdm_rpcc_get_random_bytes_full() of
.BR libesdm_rpc_client (3)):
nothing is handed to the guest before the ESDM is fully seeded, the requests
of the guest wait until it is. With
.BR \-\-pr ,
they are served from the prediction resistant DRNG, which reseeds for every
request from the entropy sources and hands out no more than they delivered.
That is the one for guests crediting the device with full entropy, as Linux
does with the input of a hardware random number generator.
.PP
While the ESDM cannot be reached, the requests of the guest wait as well, and
the daemon asks again, backing off up to every 5 seconds, until it can. It
logs that once, and once more when the ESDM serves again; what the RPC client
logs about every attempt is held back meanwhile, unless debug messages are
asked for
.RB ( \-vvvvv ).
A
request is served with at most 4096 bytes, the used length telling the driver
how many it got, and the daemon looks at its descriptors again after
65536 bytes or 256 requests in a row, so a guest asking without end does not
keep it from what the VMM tells it. That limits neither what the guest gets
nor what it costs the ESDM: the daemon serves a guest as fast as the guest
asks and the ESDM answers, as the ESDM would serve any local user reading
from it. With
.BR \-\-max_bytes ,
the bytes served are limited the way the
.B max\-bytes
and
.B period
properties of QEMU's own virtio\-rng do: a token bucket of
.I N
bytes refilled at
.I N
bytes per
.IR MS ;
a request waits until the bucket holds all of it, or as much as the bucket
holds at most.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-pr ,
every 32 bytes the guest gets are a reseed of the prediction resistant DRNG
from the entropy sources of the ESDM, which a guest asking without end would
keep busy at the expense of every other user of them. So
.B \-\-pr
limits the guest to 1024 bytes per period \- a second, unless
.B \-\-period_ms
says otherwise \- by default;
.B \-\-max_bytes
sets another limit, and
.B "\-\-max_bytes 0"
none. A Linux guest asks its hardware random number generator for 32 or
64 bytes at a time, and rarely once its own random number generator is
seeded.
.PP
The bytes pass through a buffer of the daemon on their way into guest memory,
which is excluded from core dumps and wiped after every request, as is the
memory of the guest from core dumps.
.SS The guest
Everything of the request queue in guest memory is read once, into the daemon,
and only that copy is looked at: the guest changing the memory meanwhile
changes nothing the daemon decides by. A request whose descriptors the device
may only read, that loops, runs beyond the queue size or beyond guest memory,
wraps around the address space, or has an indirect table that is not where
guest memory is, not a multiple of a descriptor, nested or longer than the
queue, is completed with nothing written and a used length of 0. A queue
whose available index moves by more than the queue size or that names a
descriptor beyond it is not processed any more until the VMM restarts it, and
the VMM is told on the error file descriptor of the queue, once per start of
the queue. A buffer may span adjacent memory regions.
.PP
The daemon offers the split virtqueue with indirect descriptors and the event
index (VIRTIO_F_VERSION_1, VIRTIO_RING_F_INDIRECT_DESC,
VIRTIO_RING_F_EVENT_IDX), and the protocol features MQ (one queue),
REPLY_ACK, RESET_DEVICE and CONFIGURE_MEM_SLOTS (up to 509 memory regions,
so memory can be plugged into the guest). A legacy driver is served as on a
little endian machine.
.SS The VMM
The daemon serves one VMM at a time. When the VMM disconnects, everything it
set up is dropped \- the memory unmapped, the queue and the negotiated features
forgotten \- and the next connection starts from scratch. A VMM that reconnects
to a restarted daemon has a request the old connection did not complete served
on the new one, also where it tells the daemon to resume at a later index, as
Cloud Hypervisor does. When the daemon ends, it stops listening before it lets
the VMM go, so a VMM reconnecting at once is refused until the next instance
listens \- or, with socket activation, waits in the socket of systemd for it.
A VMM
breaking the protocol in a way no reply can tell it \- a message of a size or
with file descriptors its request does not have, a version other than 1, a
request whose reply the daemon cannot give \- is disconnected; a request that
fails is refused with REPLY_ACK, and a request the daemon does not know is
refused or ignored. A kick file descriptor that is no eventfd \- one readable
for good, such as a file or
.I /dev/zero
\- is ignored until the VMM hands over another one.
.PP
Nothing of the protocol waits for the ESDM: stopping the queue
(GET_VRING_BASE), a reset, a new memory table or the end of the connection
take effect at once, also while a request of the guest waits to be seeded.
The index GET_VRING_BASE reports does not count that request, which is
served once the queue runs again.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-socket " \fIPATH\fR, " \-\-socket\-path=\fIPATH\fR
Listen on a UNIX domain socket at
.IR PATH ,
created with
.BR \-\-mode .
A socket left at the path by an earlier instance is replaced, unless an
instance still listens on it; any other file is not.
.TP
.B \-\-fd \fIN\fR
Serve the socket on file descriptor
.IR N :
a listening socket, or one connected to the VMM, which is served once.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-mode " \fIMODE\fR"
Access mode of the socket of
.BR \-\-socket ,
octal. The default is 0600, and 0660 with
.BR \-\-socket_group .
.TP
.B \-\-socket_group \fIGROUP\fR
Create the socket of
.B \-\-socket
with group
.I GROUP
\- the one the VMM runs with. This needs CAP_SETGID, or
.I GROUP
being the group of the daemon.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-pr
Serve the guest from the prediction resistant DRNG.
.TP
.B \-\-max_bytes \fIN\fR
Serve at most
.I N
bytes per period, see above. 0 is no limit, which is the default \- with
.B \-\-pr
the default is 1024.
.TP
.B \-\-period_ms \fIMS\fR
Length of the period of
.B \-\-max_bytes
in milliseconds, 1000 by default.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-user " \fIUSER\fR"
Started as root, the daemon drops its privileges for good once the socket is
there \- to
.IR USER ,
by default
.BR nobody .
Nothing it does afterwards needs privileges: the memory and the notifiers of
the guest come as file descriptors over the socket.
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-group " \fIGROUP\fR"
Started as root, drop to the group
.I GROUP
rather than the group of
.IR USER ,
which the daemon keeps as a supplementary group.
.TP
.B \-\-syslog
Log to syslog.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Verbose logging; repeat for more. Errors and warnings are logged in any case.
.TP
.B \-\-print\-capabilities
Print the capabilities as the vhost\-user back\-end conventions of QEMU have it
(the type "rng") and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH SYSTEMD
The template units
.B esdm\-vhost\-user\-rng@.socket
and
.B esdm\-vhost\-user\-rng@.service
run one instance per VM. The socket of VM
.I vm1
is
.IR /run/esdm\-vhost\-user\-rng/vm1.sock ,
with mode 0660 and the group of the meson option
.B vhost\-user\-rng\-socket\-group
(by default
.BR kvm );
the first connection starts the daemon:
.PP
.nf
systemctl enable \-\-now esdm\-vhost\-user\-rng@vm1.socket
.fi
.PP
The daemon runs as a user of its own
.RB ( DynamicUser= )
without capabilities, in a private network namespace and with a read\-only
file system \- the RPC socket of the ESDM is reachable regardless. A VMM
running as another user or group gets its socket with a drop\-in of the
socket unit
.RB ( "systemctl edit esdm\-vhost\-user\-rng@vm1.socket" ),
for example:
.PP
.nf
[Socket]
SocketUser=libvirt\-qemu
SocketGroup=libvirt\-qemu
SocketMode=0600
.fi
.PP
Whoever can connect to the socket controls the back\-end of the VM, so it
should be reachable by the VMM of that VM alone. Options of the daemon go into
the environment variable ESDM_VHOST_USER_RNG_OPTS of the service, for one VM or
all of them:
.PP
.nf
[Service]
Environment=ESDM_VHOST_USER_RNG_OPTS="\-\-pr \-\-max_bytes 512"
.fi
.SH QEMU
The memory of the guest has to be shared with the daemon: a memory backend
with
.BR share=on .
The device is
.B vhost\-user\-rng\-pci
on PCI:
.PP
.nf
qemu\-system\-x86_64 \-machine q35,memory\-backend=mem \-m 1G \\
	\-object memory\-backend\-memfd,id=mem,size=1G,share=on \\
	\-chardev socket,id=rng0,path=/run/esdm\-vhost\-user\-rng/vm1.sock \\
	\-device vhost\-user\-rng\-pci,chardev=rng0 ...
.fi
.PP
and
.B vhost\-user\-rng
on virtio\-mmio, e.g. on the microvm machine. microvm uses the legacy
virtio\-mmio transport unless told otherwise, which the daemon serves as well;
.B "\-global virtio\-mmio.force\-legacy=false"
gives the guest a virtio 1.0 device. The guest finds the device through ACPI,
or, with
.BR acpi=off ,
through the
.B virtio_mmio.device=
parameter QEMU adds to the kernel command line:
.PP
.nf
qemu\-system\-x86_64 \-machine microvm,memory\-backend=mem,acpi=off \-m 1G \\
	\-object memory\-backend\-memfd,id=mem,size=1G,share=on \\
	\-global virtio\-mmio.force\-legacy=false \\
	\-chardev socket,id=rng0,path=/run/esdm\-vhost\-user\-rng/vm1.sock \\
	\-device vhost\-user\-rng,chardev=rng0 \-kernel ... \-append ...
.fi
.PP
With ACPI, microvm's ACPI is hardware\-reduced, and a Linux guest then ticks
neither on the PIT nor the PIC: it needs to know the frequency of its TSC.
Under KVM, kvmclock tells it; under TCG, the guest measures the TSC against
the PIT, which fails on a busy host and leaves the guest waiting early in its
boot, before the device is ever used.
.B tsc_early_khz=
on the guest's kernel command line spares it the measurement.
.PP
QEMU (up to 11.1 at least) does not reconnect these devices to a restarted
back\-end. With
.B reconnect\-ms
on the chardev, the device fails to start ("Failed to set msg fds"): unlike
vhost\-user\-blk, it talks to the back\-end before the chardev connected. A
device hot\-plugged once the chardev is connected starts, but its reconnect
aborts QEMU in vhost_dev_start, as the device starts the vhost device it tore
down without setting it up again. So after a restart of the daemon, the device
of a running VM serves no more requests \- its guest waits \- and the next start
of the VM is served.
.PP
QEMU declares a VM with a vhost\-user\-rng device unmigratable, so the daemon
does not support migration (VHOST_F_LOG_ALL) either.
.SH CLOUD HYPERVISOR
The generic vhost\-user device of Cloud Hypervisor (52.0 and later), with the
memory shared:
.PP
.nf
cloud\-hypervisor \-\-memory size=1G,shared=on \\
	\-\-generic\-vhost\-user socket=/run/esdm\-vhost\-user\-rng/vm1.sock,device_type=rng,queue_sizes=[256] ...
.fi
.PP
Cloud Hypervisor reconnects to a restarted daemon. It retries the connection,
not the set\-up on it, though: a connection reset in the middle of the set\-up
leaves the device broken until the guest resets it.
.PP
Cloud Hypervisor has an entropy device of its own besides, which it always
gives the guest: the guest sees two virtio\-rng devices. That one reads the
file of
.B \-\-rng src=
(by default
.IR /dev/urandom );
pointing it at a device of the ESDM \-
.I /dev/esdm_urandom
of the esdm_cdev kernel module, or the device files of
.BR esdm\-cuse\-urandom (8)
\- makes both of them ESDM devices, though that one hands out bytes before the
ESDM is fully seeded. Cloud Hypervisor does not save, restore or migrate the
PCI configuration space of a generic device.
.SH THE GUEST
A Linux guest drives the device with the
.B virtio_rng
driver, which registers it as a hardware random number generator
.BR virtio_rng.N .
.I /sys/class/misc/hw_random/rng_available
lists them,
.I /sys/class/misc/hw_random/rng_current
is the one
.I /dev/hwrng
reads and the kernel feeds its entropy pool from; writing a name to it
selects another one. Which name is which device the links of
.I /sys/bus/virtio/drivers/virtio_rng/
tell, pointing to the PCI or virtio\-mmio device each virtio device sits on;
with the device of the ESDM at a known PCI address \- in Cloud Hypervisor
.B pci_device_id=
of
.B \-\-generic\-vhost\-user
\- the other one can be unbound
.RB ( "echo virtioM > /sys/bus/virtio/drivers/virtio_rng/unbind" ),
leaving the device of the ESDM the only one.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when ended by SIGTERM, SIGINT or SIGHUP, or when the VMM of
.B \-\-fd
on a connected socket went away; 1 when the daemon cannot start.
.SH EXAMPLE
Without systemd, as root, for a QEMU running as user and group
.BR qemu :
.PP
.nf
esdm\-vhost\-user\-rng \-\-socket /run/vm1\-rng.sock \-\-socket_group qemu
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR esdm\-server (8),
.BR libesdm_rpc_client (3),
.BR esdm\-cuse\-urandom (8),
.BR qemu\-system\-x86_64 (1),
.BR cloud\-hypervisor (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Markus Theil <theil.markus@gmail.com>
